O|               CONTINUED: (7)                                         
O|
N|               He leaves.                                                           
O|
S|               INT. HOUSE, ECOUST - CONTINUOUS                                      
O|
N|               He pushes through the doorway. Damaged wooden stairs lead up.        
N|               Schofield slips over the rubble, over the remains of the             
N|               house.                                                               
O|
N|               He peers along the empty street.                                     
O|
S|               EXT. SIDE STREET, ECOUST - CONTINUOUS                                
N|               Schofield stays in the shadows, and begins to work his way           
N|               along the street in the direction Lauri pointed him.                 
O|
N|               The town is silent. He looks up at the sky to see if the sun         
N|               is rising. No light.                                                 
O|
N|               He reaches a crossroads - small alleyways branch off. He             
N|               looks around. Lost. He turns to his right - a wide alleyway.         
O|
N|               He moves along it, quickly, quietly.                                 
O|
N|               BANG - ahead of him a door flies open, warm light spills out         
N,I|               onto the street, followed by a German soldier. PRIVATE               
N,I|               MULLER, 30s, blind drunk.                                            
N|               Muller stumbles a few steps and then vomits. Moaning and             
N|               muttering to himself.                                                
O|
N|               Schofield ducks into the darkness of a doorway.                      
O|
N|               Muller moans and pukes again. Schofield backs inside the             
N|               doorway and into-                                                    
O|
S|               INT. SCHOOL HOUSE, ECOUST - CONTINUOUS                               
N|               A small school assembly hall and a couple of other rooms have        
N|               been blown together into one large space. Metal beams where          
N|               there once was a roof. A few school desks, tipped onto their         
N|               sides.                                                               
O|
N|               To one side of the room, wide arched windows let in shafts of        
N|               light from the burning church outside. They streak across the        
N|               inky darkness.                                                       
O|
N|               At the far end of the room is the door Private Muller just           
N|               exited. A small fire burns on the floor by it. Smoke hangs in        
N|               the room. A couple of empty bottles lie around.                      
O|
O|                                                                (CONTINUED)
O|
O|
O|                                                                     89.        
O|
O|               CONTINUED:                                                
O|
N|               Schofield stays in the shadows. Silently scanning the                
N|               darkness, listening to the pathetic moans of Muller outside.         
N|               He looks around, searching for another way out, a way past           
N|               Muller.                                                              
O|
N,I|               Out of the shadows steps a man - ANOTHER GERMAN SOLDIER -            
N,I|               BAUMER, late teens. He is doing up his flies.                        
O|
N|               They lock eyes, three feet apart.                                    
O|
N|               A beat - shock on both their faces, then horror. Neither of          
N|               them want this.                                                      
N|               Baumer opens his mouth to scream.                                    
O|
N|               Schofield closes the three feet and is on him - pushing              
N|               Baumer hard against a pillar.                                        
O|
N|               Schofield holds Baumer there, his hand clamped over the young        
N|               soldiers mouth. They lock eyes. Schofield holds his finger to        
N|               his lips: Stay quiet.                                                
O|
N|               Baumer nods.                                                         
O|
N|               Schofield slowly drops his hand from Baumer’s mouth. Wary.           
N|               Baumer sucks in a breath and shouts out:                             
O|
C|                          BAUMER                         BAUMER                     
U|               Engländer!                     English!                              
O|
N|               Schofield reacts quickly, ramming his palm into Baumer’s             
N|               mouth, gagging him as they both fall onto the hard ground -          
N|               the sound echoes loudly though the school house.                     
O|
N|               Baumer bites down on Schofield’s hand, still bound with its          
N|               bandage.                                                             
O|
N|               Schofield gasps out, gritting his teeth against the pain. He         
N|               forces his hand further into Baumer’s mouth, his other hand          
N|               goes to the boys throat - squeezing with all his strength.           
N|               Baumer has a knife. Schofield wrestles it out of his hand.           
O|
N|               Baumer thrashes and kicks under Schofield, rolling the two of        
N|               them to the side.                                                    
O|
N|               They are two feral creatures - both know this is to the              
N|               death.                                                               
O|
N|               A shadow at the far end of the room. Muller is coming back...        
O|
O|                                                                (CONTINUED)
O|
O|
O|                                                                     90.        
O|
O|               CONTINUED: (2)                                         
O|
C|                      PRIVATE MULLER                 PRIVATE MULLER                 
U|               Mein Gott Baumer... Das war    Christ Baumer... This was a           
U|               ein Fehler. Wir sollten heute  mistake. We should go back            
U|               Abend zurück gehen.            tonight, maybe no one will            
U|               Vielleicht hat niemand         notice we’ve gone.                    
U|               gemerkt, dass wir weg waren.                                         
N|               Muller staggers his way over to a spot by the fire, slumps           
N|               down. Rummages among the empty bottles.                              
O|
N|               Baumer tears a breath in through his nostrils, tries to              
N|               scream -                                                             
O|
N|               Schofield squeezes harder on the boy’s neck, pushing the             
N|               boy’s head down into the ground. Crushing him into the broken        
N|               glass and debris.                                                    
C,E|                      PRIVATE MULLER (CONT'D)        PRIVATE MULLER (CONT'D)        
U|               Wo ist der Brandy? Du kleiner  Where’s the brandy? You               
U|               Scheisser.. wehe du bist       little shit, you better not           
U|               damit fortgelaufen.            have run off with it.                 
O|
N|               Desperately, Baumer beats his hands against Schofield’s              
N|               chest. Muller hears the noise.                                       
O|
N|               Muller turns and peers towards them. He can’t see them in the        
N|               shadows.                                                             
C,E|                      PRIVATE MULLER (CONT'D)        PRIVATE MULLER (CONT'D)        
U|               Baumer. Wo ist..? Baumer?      Baumer. Where is..? Baumer?           
O|
N|               But Schofield can see him, and his focus briefly shifts.             
O|
N|               In that moment, Baumer fights back - Kicking, clawing,               
N|               punching. But he is weaker now. Schofield redoubles his              
N|               efforts. His hands and arms ache. Acid stings in his muscles.        
O|
N|               Schofield is desperate. His eyes flick between Muller and            
N|               Baumer.                                                              
N|               Baumer’s feet scratch and scrape frantically on the stone.           
O|
C,E|                      PRIVATE MULLER (CONT'D)        PRIVATE MULLER (CONT'D)        
U|               Baumer?                        Baumer?                               
O|
N|               Muller stands, teeters towards them.                                 
O|
N|               Then - Baumer’s arms fall limp.                                      
O|
N|               Schofield snaps up, leaving his rifle behind him, and leaps          
N|               out of the shadows.                                                  
O|
O|                                                                (CONTINUED)
O|
O|
O|                                                                     91.        
O|
O|               CONTINUED: (3)                                         
O|
N|               He barges straight past Muller, heading for the door at the          
N|               far end of the room. Muller staggers back -                          
C,E|                                   MULLER (O.S.)                                    
U|                         BAUMER!                                                    
O|
S|               EXT. SIDE STREET, ECOUST - CONTINUOUS                                
O|
N|               Schofield is out of the door, running, hands free, breath            
N|               sawing in his ears. The sound of Muller behind him-                  
O|
C,E|                   PRIVATE MULLER (O.S.)          PRIVATE MULLER (O.S.)             
U|               ENGLANDER! ENGLANDER!          ENGLISH! ENGLISH!                     
N|               Muller’s howl chases Schofield along the street. The sound of        
N|               the door swinging open echoes after him.                             
O|
N|               Schofield doesn’t stop. Behind him is the sound of Muller            
N|               giving chase then-                                                   
O|
N|               CRACK-                                                               
O|
N|               A bullet sings off the wall opposite.                                
O|
S|               EXT. SMALL STREET, ECOUST - CONTINUOUS                               
N|               Schofield sprints, looking over his shoulder.                        
O|
N|               He turns... and fifty yards in front him -                           
O|
N|               Another German Soldier. The same man who chased him across           
N|               the square. The soldier breaks into a run, reaches for his           
N|               rifle.                                                               
O|
N|               The soldier shoots, but Schofield breaks left across the             
N|               street and into -                                                    
O|
S|               EXT. TINY STREET, ECOUST - CONTINUOUS                                
O|
N|               A narrow alley.                                                      
O|
N|               Schofield darts down it, looking for some escape.                    
O|
N|               Muller and the other German race into the alley behind him.          
O|
N|               Shots burst on the wall next to Schofield.                           
O|
N|               To his left is another corner, Schofield sprints for it. Flat        
N|               out into -                                                           
O|
O|
O|                                                                     92.        
O|
S|               EXT. CURVED STAIRS STREET, ECOUST - CONTINUOUS                       
N|               Ahead of Schofield is a flight of stone steps.                       
O|
N|               He leaps down. Taking them three or four at a time.                  
O|
N|               He slams against the wall, leaps down another flight.                
O|
N|               His breath burns in his lungs. At the bottom of the stairs,          
N|               is a long straight street, about 100 yards. Leading to a             
N|               Bridge.                                                              
O|
N|               Schofield runs towards it.                                           
O|
S|               EXT. BRIDGE STREET, ECOUST - CONTINUOUS                              
O|
N|               His heartbeat thunders as he sprints flat out.                       
O|
N,G|               The street slopes downhill. Yards fall under him. We can now         
N,G|               see the burning Town receding behind him.                            
O|
N|               The sound of the Two Germans battering down the stairs echoes        
N|               after him.                                                           
O|
N|               They hit the flat street. 30 yards behind him.                       
N|               They open fire.                                                      
O|
N|               Bullets crack off the cobbles just in front of him.                  
O|
N|               He is 50 yards from the bridge, running full tilt.                   
O|
N|               Shots ring out.                                                      
O|
N|               Schofield reaches the bridge. Bullets ring off the rubble.           
O|
N|               He keeps sprinting.                                                  
O|
N|               And suddenly, with no warning, Schofield veers across the            
N|               street, puts one hand on the stone wall of the bridge...             
N|               ...and vaults clean over it -                                        
O|
S|               EXT. RIVER, ECOUST - CONTINUOUS                                      
O|
N|               Schofield drops forty feet and smashes into the water.               
O|
N,G|               We are under the dark water.                                         
O|
O|                                                                (CONTINUED)
O|
O|
O|                                                                     93.        
O|
O|               CONTINUED:                                                
O|
N|               Schofield resurfaces, gasping for air. Numb, panicking, he           
N|               thrashes and kicks, fighting the weight, the cold, the fear.         
N|               He struggles to slip off his webbing. It tangles up in his           
N|               arms, pulling him down.                                              
O|
N|               Finally he gets it off, it is swallowed up in the white water        
N|               which churns all around him.                                         
O|
N|               Losing the weight of the webbing, he manages to stay above           
N|               the water.                                                           
O|
N|               The bridge and the German soldiers are long gone. The water          
N|               is fast flowing after the rain, rapids sweep him along.              
N|               A felled tree lies across the river. Schofield grabs hold of         
N|               its branches, tries to pull himself up. But the torrent is           
N|               too strong to fight. It rips his hands away, and pulls him           
N|               under again.                                                         
O|
N|               He surfaces. Coughs and splutters on the water line.                 
O|
N|               The rapids continue to pull him through the water at speed.          
O|
N|               Schofield goes with it. Letting the water carry him.                 
O|
N|               He scans the river banks ahead. They tower up steeply. He            
N|               looks for a way out. Around him, rocks jut out from the water        
N|               dangerously. They churn the river into a whirlpool, spinning         
N|               him around.                                                          
N|               He is now traveling backwards downstream.                            
O|
N|               Behind him, a large rock rises up out of the water. He is            
N|               approaching it fast. He doesn’t see it.                              
O|
N|               The water drives him hard into the rock. His back and head           
N|               are slammed against it.                                              
O|
N|               Schofield is winded, disorientated, barely staying afloat.           
N|               Ahead there is a sound.                                              
O|
N|               A deep rumbling.                                                     
O|
N|               He fights to keep his ears above the water, to hear it.              
O|
N|               The rapids are getting faster, more turbulent.                       
O|
N|               Schofield realizes what the sound is - the roar of water.            
O|
O|                                                                (CONTINUED)
O|
O|
O|                                                                     94.        
O|
O|               CONTINUED: (2)                                         
O|
N|               Panic flashes on his face. He thrashes, tries to swim to a           
N|               bank.                                                                
N|               But it’s too late.                                                   
O|
N|               A waterfall lies ahead.                                              
O|
N|               He braces himself, and then the waterfall is on him in a             
N|               flash.                                                               
O|
N|               Schofield goes over it.                                              
O|
N|               He is pulled down into the plunge pool.                              
N,G|               We lose sight of him. And then, nothing. He is gone.                 
O|
N|               For a few moments, just the roar of the falls.                       
O|
N|               Then, suddenly Schofield resurfaces, gasping for air.                
O|
N|               The churning water pushes him free of the falls. He manages          
N|               to turn onto his back.                                               
O|
N|               The river has got wider, deeper. He grabs hold of a branch.          
O|
N|               The current carries him. The world around him has turned blue        
N|               in the pre-dawn light.                                               
N|               The river sweeps him forward. He is still gasping for breath.        
O|
N|               Now the tumbling river gradually begins to smooth out into a         
N|               cool apron of water.                                                 
O|
N|               Schofield is almost unconscious. He is slipping down, his            
N|               mouth just above the waterline.                                      
O|
N|               His eyes flutter and open, he spits out water.                       
O|
N|               Grey mistrals roll through a pale world. Unearthly. They             
N|               hover above the river ahead.                                         
N|               The river slowly pulls him. Occasional trees line the bank.          
O|
N|               This place is untouched by war. Spared. Clean and cool and           
N|               filled with some life.                                               
O|
N|               Schofield is fighting it, but ready to accept that this is           
N|               the end. He knows too well there are worse places, worse             
N|               ways...                                                              
O|
N|               Inch by inch he starts to slip down.                                 
O|
O|                                                                (CONTINUED)
O|
O|
O|                                                                     95.        
O|
O|               CONTINUED: (3)                                         
O|
N|               His ears fall under the waterline. The sound is sucked from          
N|               the world. His eyes stare upwards. Lips just above the water.        
N|               Schofield seems so peaceful, just floating on the water. The         
N|               sound of the falls recedes.                                          
O|
N|               We are aware of birds, the wind in the leaves.                       
O|
N|               Then the water around him turns flat. The current begins to          
N|               slow.                                                                
O|
N|               White. Petals float on it, a patchwork blanket.                      
O|
N|               Cherry Blossom.                                                      
N|               Schofield is swept through the white petals.                         
O|
N|               Schofield raises an arm from the water and sees the petals           
N|               clinging to him.                                                     
O|
N|               Blake.                                                               
O|
N|               A long beat.                                                         
O|
N|               Life seeps back into him, breaks through the icy numbness.           
O|
N|               Schofield’s limbs struggle to work in the cold.                      
N|               He fights, willing movement.                                         
O|
N|               He swims towards the bank.                                           
O|
N|               Ahead there are the sounds of a dam: a gentle fountain of            
N|               water.                                                               
O|
N|               The sun is rising somewhere - the pre-dawn light is beginning        
N|               to illuminate the world around him.                                  
O|
N|               He has reached the dam - a fallen tree. He begins to haul            
N|               himself out. He looks down.                                          
N|               BODIES.                                                              
O|
N|               Twelve bodies, give or take.                                         
O|
N|               SOLDIERS - British, German. And CIVILIANS. Men and women.            
O|
N|               They have caught and gathered, blocked by the tree from              
N|               floating downstream. They have formed a kind of dam.                 
O|
N|               He takes the only option. He pulls himself up, and climbs            
N|               across the bodies. His way out.                                      
O|
O|                                                                (CONTINUED)
O|
O|
O|                                                                     96.        
O|
